					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/buttfranklin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2721810); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2721810&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608627809" alt="Buttfranklin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2721810)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/buttfranklin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2721810); return false;" >Buttfranklin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1475736479 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">06 Oct 2016 06:47</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2666068">
									

<p>This was a good, enjoyable read. You manage to make individual voices sing out in the dialogue, while <em>also</em> making it sound pretty realistic, which not many folks here can do well. The narrative is put together well, in that you've chosen a nice order to present the information in to keep us readers hooked from one section to the next. Oh, plus it's super creepy.</p>
<p>An easy +1, but with some little criticisms.</p>
<ul>
<li>I'll agree that some of those addenda aren't doing much for the story. You could whittle away a few of those superfluous letters.</li>
<li>You maybe gave us <em>too much</em> mystery in the initial section of the description, especially with regards to what makes this thing anomalous. It's very vague. I totally get that you don't want to give everything away so we'll feel compelled to keep reading, but my response to that first bit was closer to confusion and slight frustration than being hooked in.</li>
<li>For a piece this long you have some of the inevitable grammary problemos, e.g. &quot;fucks&quot; when you should have had &quot;fuck's [sake]&quot;. I also would quibble a few word choices, e.g. I'm not sure I would use &quot;disparate&quot; in that context, and I'm not sure I'd begin a footnote with &quot;which&quot;. (But these are minor concerns, and the &quot;&quot;&quot;tone&quot;&quot;&quot; overall is pretty bang-on.)</li>
<li>edit: containment procedures were quite bog-standard, but you get them out of the way quickly so they don't detract too much from the rest.</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
